<section image-layout="blank" id="7eRYTYUjqB" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="QcCKadrukz" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">Quarterly business review</p><h1 font-size="44pt">Q3 FY26 Business Review</h1><p font-size="lg">Results, causes, and three decisions that change the next two quarters.</p><spacer size="24pt"></spacer><p font-size="sm">Kestrel Systems · Leadership team · Illustrative worked example</p></grid-item><grid-item id="9f8KhfVAq7" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/ctm0G56-QMDh0FeGHcTsu.png" saved-media-id="9fgch4luq85ppsl" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="causal-model-studio" alt="Editorial studio photograph of a functional tabletop operating model assembled from matte warm-white card and machined aluminum, a graphite rail branching through several gates toward one signal-red intervention lever and a restrained green endpoint, crisp directional light, no text, lettering, screens, logos, or people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="29hy11ZwyR"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="ToOCC2uEeX" background-color="surface" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Read this first</p><display size="md">Fictional by design</display><p>Kestrel Systems is fictional. Every figure, customer, and quote was invented to demonstrate the structure of a leadership QBR.</p><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="sm"><p font-size="sm"><b>Profile:</b> vertical SaaS for mid-market food and beverage manufacturers · $48.2M ARR · 212 employees · Series C</p></grid-item><grid-item id="eZnA6IPldQ" colspan="40"><h2>A worked example, not real company data</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" marker="number" marker-position="inline" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" direction="vertical" style="--marker-inline-size: 32pt; --marker-gap: 14pt;"><grid-item id="2M_-QrnO7z" padding="14pt 0"><h4>One dataset runs the whole deck</h4><p>The ARR bridge, segment ledger, account counts, and retention rates all reconcile to the same beginning-of-quarter book.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="nQ-_6bJA-u" padding="14pt 0"><h4>Three kinds of statement stay distinct</h4><p>Fictional dataset figures, assumptions used to project, and the real, cited industry benchmarks on slide 5.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="xV6hp6ILlu" padding="14pt 0"><h4>The point is the operating argument</h4><p>The deck moves from the miss, to its cause, to interventions with owners and accountable numbers.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="q9F_fNFJuh"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="BnnWLkKX3J"><h2>We missed by $0.7M, and expansion covered most of a $0.9M new-logo hole</h2><p>Q3 closed at <b>$48.2M ARR</b> against a $48.9M plan. New logo was the whole problem: $2.1M against $3.0M. Expansion beat plan by $0.6M and absorbed two thirds of that shortfall.</p><table col-widths="[40,20,20,20]" header-row="true" borders="rows" striped="false" style="--table-cell-padding: 2pt 12pt;"><tbody><tr><td><p>Q3 FY26 ARR bridge ($M)</p></td><td><p>Plan</p></td><td><p>Actual</p></td><td><p>Variance</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Beginning ARR (Jul 1)</p></td><td><p>44.9</p></td><td><p>44.9</p></td><td><p>0.0</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>New logo</p></td><td><p>3.0</p></td><td><p>2.1</p></td><td><p><span text-color="#B8321E">-0.9</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Expansion</p></td><td><p>2.0</p></td><td><p>2.6</p></td><td><p><span text-color="palette-2">+0.6</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Contraction</p></td><td><p>-0.4</p></td><td><p>-0.5</p></td><td><p><span text-color="#B8321E">-0.1</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Gross churn</p></td><td><p>-0.6</p></td><td><p>-0.9</p></td><td><p><span text-color="#B8321E">-0.3</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Closing ARR (Sep 30)</b></p></td><td><p><b>48.9</b></p></td><td><p><b>48.2</b></p></td><td><p><span text-color="#B8321E"><b>-0.7</b></span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><aside variant="caution"><p>Churn and contraction totaled $1.4M versus a $1.0M plan. Half came from SMB, only 14% of the book. The decision is whether we keep selling to a segment we cannot keep.</p></aside></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="GgBY3-AdSd"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="RNI-TdY3KY"><h2>The quarter in five numbers</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="Tvehop_GCc" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><graph chart-type="waterfall" show-data-labels="true" text-size="md" width="500pt" height="285pt"><properties><property key="component" label="Component" type="label"></property><property key="arr" label="ARR ($M)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Q2 closing ARR</cell><cell>44.9</cell></row><row><cell>New logo</cell><cell>2.1</cell></row><row><cell>Expansion</cell><cell>2.6</cell></row><row><cell>Contraction</cell><cell>-0.5</cell></row><row><cell>Gross churn</cell><cell>-0.9</cell></row><row><cell>Q3 closing ARR</cell><cell>48.2</cell></row></data></graph><p role="caption">Expansion did the work new logos were supposed to do; churn and contraction took back $1.4M.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Xmqtsy6O18" colspan="25"><grid divider="sm neutral-border" direction="vertical"><grid-item id="-vjh62cuWo" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>$48.2M ARR</b><br><span text-color="muted">+7.3% on Q2; +29% year over year</span></p></grid-item><grid-item id="AbXjl68Bjd" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>96.9% quarterly GRR</b><br><span text-color="muted">88% annualized; down from 91%</span></p></grid-item><grid-item id="FOdAL5lrOx" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>102.7% quarterly NRR</b><br><span text-color="muted">111% annualized; down from 114%</span></p></grid-item><grid-item id="X6YA6ANYxr" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>$2.40 per $1 of new ARR</b><br><span text-color="muted">Sales and marketing; up from $1.95</span></p></grid-item><grid-item id="uvI_ZrN9Wc" padding="9pt 0"><p><b>19 months of runway</b><br><span text-color="muted">$31M cash; 1.5× burn multiple</span></p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="j_JmLn9Dvw"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="Y0URhINrQ0"><h2>Growth is ahead of the market; retention is behind it</h2><p>Kestrel's annualized figures against published 2025 private B2B SaaS medians.</p><table col-widths="[25,17,26,32]" header-row="true" borders="rows" striped="false"><tbody><tr><td><p>Metric</p></td><td><p>Kestrel</p></td><td><p>2025 benchmark</p></td><td><p>Read</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>ARR growth</p></td><td><p><b>29%</b></p></td><td><p>26% median</p></td><td><p><span text-color="palette-2">Ahead of market</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Net revenue retention</p></td><td><p><b>111%</b></p></td><td><p>101% median</p></td><td><p>The blended number leadership tends to quote</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Gross revenue retention</p></td><td><p><b>88%</b></p></td><td><p>91% median; 90–91% below $250k ACV</p></td><td><p><span text-color="palette-1">Below market on whether customers stay</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>New-customer CAC ratio</p></td><td><p><b>$2.40</b></p></td><td><p>$2.00 median</p></td><td><p><span text-color="palette-1">A fifth less efficient</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Expansion share of new ARR</p></td><td><p><b>55%</b><br>$2.6M of $4.7M</p></td><td><p>40% industry-wide; above 50% over $50M ARR</p></td><td><p>Already living off the installed base</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://www.benchmarkit.ai/2025benchmarks">Benchmarkit 2025 SaaS Performance Metrics</a> · <a href="https://www.saas-capital.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RB32WS1-2025-B2B-SaaS-Retention-Benchmarks.pdf">SaaS Capital 2025 B2B SaaS Retention Benchmarks</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="7QSWNExStw"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="L3ZJPyTdsm"><h2>NRR is a blended number, and blending is hiding the problem</h2><p font-size="sm">Quarterly rates use the $44.9M book at July 1. Gross retention subtracts churn and contraction; net adds expansion. Annualized rates elsewhere compound the quarterly rate four times and assume it holds.</p><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="0" color-scheme="theme" text-size="md" width="850pt" height="158pt"><properties><property key="segment" label="Segment" type="label"></property><property key="gross" label="Gross retention (%)" type="data"></property><property key="net" label="Net retention (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Enterprise</cell><cell>98.9</cell><cell>106.2</cell></row><row><cell>Mid-market</cell><cell>96.4</cell><cell>100.9</cell></row><row><cell>SMB</cell><cell>88.9</cell><cell>90.5</cell></row></data></graph><table col-widths="[18,12,13,17,16,12,12]" header-row="true" borders="rows" striped="false"><tbody><tr><td><p font-size="11pt">Segment</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">Customers</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">Avg ACV</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">Beginning ARR</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">Churn + contraction</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">Expansion</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">Ending ARR</p></td></tr><tr><td><p font-size="11pt">Enterprise</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">137</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">$200k</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">27.4</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">-0.30</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">2.00</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">29.10</p></td></tr><tr><td><p font-size="11pt">Mid-market</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">224</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">$50k</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">11.2</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">-0.40</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">0.50</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">11.30</p></td></tr><tr><td><p font-size="11pt">SMB</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">420</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">$15k</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">6.3</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">-0.70</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">0.10</p></td><td><p font-size="11pt">5.70</p></td></tr><tr><td><p font-size="11pt"><b>Total</b></p></td><td><p font-size="11pt"><b>781</b></p></td><td><p font-size="11pt"><b>$57k</b></p></td><td><p font-size="11pt"><b>44.9</b></p></td><td><p font-size="11pt"><b>-1.40</b></p></td><td><p font-size="11pt"><b>2.60</b></p></td><td><p font-size="11pt"><b>46.10</b></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p role="caption"><span text-color="#5E6875"><b>SMB net retention is below 100%.</b> Enterprise expansion is the only reason the blended 102.7% looks healthy.</span></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="NRxr69ZLbg"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="rCbcx78r7L"><h2>SMB is 14% of revenue and 54% of the logos</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="ETrQ8v7A2l" vertical-align="center" colspan="30"><display size="lg">14%</display><h4>of beginning ARR</h4><p>420 customers at $15k average ACV.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="LBEoE5EonW" vertical-align="center" colspan="30"><display size="lg">54%</display><h4>of 781 logos</h4><p>46% of support tickets and 38% of CSM hours.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="PMxjv1Eyd1" colspan="20"><h3>$5.1k</h3><h4>Cost to serve per account</h4><p font-size="sm">Against a $15k contract, leaving about $9.9k of annual contribution.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Ot6dEvyQfq" colspan="20"><h3>$37k vs $18k</h3><h4>Assumed lifetime contribution vs acquisition cost</h4><p font-size="sm">31 churns, 7.4% quarterly, imply 26% annually and a 3.8-year life if held constant. That is 2.1×; the 2024 cohort was below 1.0×.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="n5mFUVRfpf" colspan="20"><h3>$1.2M</h3><h4>Six enterprise expansions</h4><p font-size="sm">About $200k each. Every one was worth more than a dozen new SMB logos.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="kFFkzkjf5s"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="z2WRJWVBJr"><h2>Why SMB customers leave: we asked the last 40</h2><grid spacing="large"><grid-item id="HNXryYGwCa" vertical-align="center" colspan="37"><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="0" text-size="md" width="570pt" height="300pt"><properties><property key="cause" label="Cause" type="label"></property><property key="accounts" label="Churned accounts" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Never got live</cell><cell>17</cell></row><row><cell>Lost their champion</cell><cell>11</cell></row><row><cell>Price at renewal</cell><cell>7</cell></row><row><cell>Product gaps</cell><cell>5</cell></row></data></graph><p role="caption"><span text-color="muted">Last 40 SMB accounts to churn across two quarters.</span></p></grid-item><grid-item id="ljnYeEDY3f" vertical-align="center" colspan="23"><display size="lg">70%</display><h3>came from the top two causes</h3><p>Seventeen never got live: under 20% of seats activated and 51 days to first production work order. Eleven lost the original champion.</p><p>Seven left over renewal pricing; five cited product gaps, four of them multi-site reporting.</p><aside variant="caution"><p>Neither leading cause is about the product. Both are about the first sixty days.</p></aside></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="D2md-o9qkK"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="vzYKGu-wdp"><h2>Onboarding is the single highest-leverage thing we are bad at</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="Kh76f_tRdi" colspan="15"><h4>Capacity starts thin</h4><p>Three implementation engineers serve 781 customers.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="5lGDA6psZz" colspan="15"><h4>The queue forms</h4><p>Every deal over $60k waits behind another deal.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="vVKxjCLMDR" colspan="15"><h4>SMB gets self-service</h4><p>The cohort least able to self-serve receives the least help.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="fAHYxI_OvI" colspan="15"><h4>Retention pays the bill</h4><p>The staffing decision has been deferred for four quarters.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer size="18pt"></spacer><grid divider="sm neutral-border"><grid-item id="XBdfF__g2U" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md">51 / 44</display><h4>Days to first production use</h4><p>Enterprise / SMB. Target: 30 days.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="QW3lp3lfOc" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md"><span text-color="palette-2">94%</span></display><h4>Renewal when live inside 30 days</h4><p>The curve rewards speed.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="AIJAnFEcer" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md"><span text-color="palette-1">67%</span></display><h4>Renewal after more than 60 days</h4><p>The same product, delivered too late.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="sNdl0rl8x_"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="-be1jsxyNj"><h2>What went right, and it was not small</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="tHqk1JnVV1" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h3><span text-color="palette-2">$4.1M ARR</span></h3><h4>Downtime Analytics</h4><p>Reached $4.1M in its fourth quarter, attached to 34% of enterprise accounts. Fastest module ramp in company history.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="zFi2Z5lu_A" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h3><span text-color="palette-2">2 × $400k+</span></h3><h4>Enterprise expansions</h4><p>A dairy group expanded from 3 to 11 plants; a bakery co-op added compliance reporting. Both sit inside $2.0M of enterprise expansion.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Fzwe1aVbv0" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h3><span text-color="palette-2">141 → 118 days</span></h3><h4>Enterprise sales cycle</h4><p>Security review moved to the front of the process and removed 23 days.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="7zmp8ZH1JF" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h3><span text-color="palette-2">$198k → $227k</span></h3><h4>ARR per employee</h4><p>Improved on flat headcount since March.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="LZBIPFd-ZL"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="e-w-pcpSHs"><h2>The Q4 pipeline does not currently support the plan</h2><grid spacing="large"><grid-item id="OoQjNlXSJA" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="0" color-scheme="alternating" text-size="md" width="500pt" height="285pt"><properties><property key="coverage" label="Pipeline coverage" type="label"></property><property key="multiple" label="Coverage (×)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Qualified now</cell><cell>2.7</cell></row><row><cell>Required at 29% win rate</cell><cell>3.5</cell></row></data></graph><p role="caption">$9.2M qualified pipeline against a $3.4M new-logo plan.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="5-Rcz9Tg0I" vertical-align="center" colspan="25"><labels><label variant="solid">$2.7M pipeline gap</label></labels><h3>About five weeks of top-of-funnel at current create rates</h3><hr color="neutral-border"><p><b>$2.8M at renewal:</b> three accounts over $500k land in Q4. A 14-plant protein processor is amber on multi-site reporting.</p><p><b>$740k deal exposed:</b> committed EU data residency must ship in December. A slip also costs credibility with two other prospects.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="IeHbHZlf7W"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="O-0Uk-NKga"><h2>Three decisions we need from this room</h2><p>Each has an owner, a cost, and a number it is accountable for.</p><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" spacing="medium" padding-top="28pt"><grid-item id="Zg4p17vzx9" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h3>Reallocate six CSMs</h3><p font-size="sm">Move pooled SMB coverage to named enterprise accounts. Net headcount change: zero. SMB moves to in-app onboarding and tech-touch.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Accountable number:</b> enterprise gross retention from 98.9% to 99.4% quarterly, or 96% to 98% annualized.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="vTzz2eKlz8" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h3>Reset SMB pricing</h3><p font-size="sm">Raise the floor from $9k to $14k and launch a $3,600 self-serve tier. About 40 accounts average $10k.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Standing cost:</b> lose $400k ARR, save $204k of service cost, and give up roughly $196k of contribution each year. It does not pay for itself.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Accountable number:</b> 90-day conversion mix and capacity released.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="PczNlYACaM" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h3>Fund four implementation engineers</h3><p font-size="sm">$620k annualized. The hires attack the queue rather than another symptom.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Accountable number:</b> median time to first use from 51 days to 30 by the end of Q1.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="pJglpw0VOf"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="2n7JcHzIy3"><h2>What changes if we do all three</h2><p>Modeled outcomes, with the assumption that the observed retention curve continues to hold.</p><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="qpdYE6nluz" cell-style="subtle" padding="14pt 18pt" colspan="30"><display size="md" font-size="40pt"><span text-color="palette-2">88% → 92%</span></display><h4>Annualized gross retention by Q2 FY27</h4><p>Driven by named enterprise coverage and faster onboarding.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="3pv2Xi2ZbA" cell-style="subtle" padding="14pt 18pt" colspan="30"><display size="md" font-size="40pt"><span text-color="palette-2">$2.40 → $2.05</span></display><h4>New-customer CAC ratio</h4><p>We stop paying full sales cost to win $15k contracts.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="GNgic0X0KH" cell-style="subtle" padding="14pt 18pt" colspan="30"><display size="md" font-size="40pt"><span text-color="palette-3">781 → ~740</span></display><h4>Logo count falls about 5%</h4><p>ARR growth dips to ~24% for two quarters, then reaccelerates. Decide today to tell the board.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="2nCe6GwvtD" cell-style="subtle" padding="14pt 18pt" colspan="30"><display size="md" font-size="40pt"><span text-color="palette-4">$816k / year</span></display><h4>Net new cost</h4><p>$620k payroll + ~$196k contribution foregone. Four retention points on $50M ≈ $2.0M/year—modeled, not committed.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="9jMICenlEe"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="Gv2DpAS4Ph"><h2>What we are accepting if we are wrong</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="uwc3drgMHK" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow"><span text-color="palette-3">Exposure 01</span></p><h3>Self-serve cannibalizes mid-market</h3><p>Feature gate at three sites and review conversion mix in 90 days. If conversion is weak, the floor remains a $196k-a-year revenue decision with only capacity to show for it.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="qHu6wCe4up" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow"><span text-color="palette-3">Exposure 02</span></p><h3>Implementation hires ramp slowly</h3><p>True. The retention benefit lands in Q2 FY27, not Q4.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="7SaXVphrNV" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow"><span text-color="palette-3">Exposure 03</span></p><h3>SMB is a farm system</h3><p>Partly true: 9 of 224 mid-market customers started in SMB. The self-serve tier keeps that path open at a tenth of the cost.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="PlIFmBUBL5" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow"><span text-color="palette-3">Exposure 04</span></p><h3>The lifetime math assumes steady churn</h3><p>If SMB churn is accelerating, every payback figure on slide 7 is optimistic and the case for the price floor gets stronger.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="qmCVV_e7Jv" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="Wh1J9oYqzo" colspan="38"><h2>Decisions, owners &amp; next checkpoint</h2><grid divider="sm palette-5" direction="vertical"><grid-item id="IbUlSkGDnB" padding="8pt 0"><h4>CSM reallocation · VP Customer Success</h4><p font-size="sm">Decision today · Effective November 1</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Gmc0shmzXx" padding="8pt 0"><h4>SMB pricing floor and self-serve · CRO with Finance</h4><p font-size="sm">Decision today · In market January 15</p></grid-item><grid-item id="6weJK7s9uX" padding="8pt 0"><h4>Implementation hiring · VP Services</h4><p font-size="sm">Approve budget today · All four seated by December 31</p></grid-item><grid-item id="ZKs3b8Ebp_" padding="8pt 0"><h4>Q4 pipeline gap · CRO</h4><p font-size="sm">$2.7M plan back to this group in two weeks</p></grid-item></grid><spacer size="16pt"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="txm5UN1Wy3" background-color="surface" text-color="heading" padding="12pt 16pt"><p role="eyebrow">Next checkpoint · November 15</p><p>Monthly retention review begins, with time to first use reported as a headline metric alongside ARR.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item><grid-item id="jHb50Dh8u4" padding="0" colspan="22"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/OIG-e351jaU3Wazg6_bJT.png" saved-media-id="ny0tqhj4a8eqbzk" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="causal-model-studio" alt="Editorial studio photograph of four machined aluminum control levers aligned beside a matte graphite operating rail, each lever feeding one shared checkpoint gate marked only by a restrained green mechanical indicator and one signal-red decision pin, warm-white card base, crisp directional light, no text, lettering, screens, logos, or people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>